# MASTER DEVELOPMENT PROMPT — LAST SIGNAL
## Build a Premium Realistic 3D Sci-Fi Rover Exploration Game

You are a senior game developer, 3D environment artist, React Three Fiber specialist, gameplay engineer, and UI/UX designer.

Your task is to design and develop a complete, polished, playable browser-based 3D game named **LAST SIGNAL**.

Do not just create a visual prototype or a basic Three.js scene. Build an actual playable game with working mechanics, a proper mission system, a realistic 3D environment, an immersive interface, and carefully structured code.

You have full responsibility for implementing the project in the current workspace.

---

# 1. GAME VISION

**Game title:** LAST SIGNAL

**Mission:** 01 — THE LOST FACILITY

**Genre:** Third-person 3D exploration, survival, and vehicle simulation.

**Platform:** Desktop web browser, with responsive support for smaller screens.

**Visual direction:** Realistic sci-fi exploration with cinematic environmental lighting, industrial design, realistic materials, atmospheric fog, detailed models, and immersive sound.

### Core story

The year is 2089.

A remote scientific research facility located in an isolated desert region has suddenly stopped transmitting signals.

The facility was conducting experiments involving an advanced energy system. Communication was lost after an unexplained incident.

An autonomous rescue rover has been deployed to investigate the facility.

The player controls this rover.

The mission is to enter the abandoned research facility, explore the surrounding environment, locate three missing energy cores, avoid environmental hazards, and reactivate the emergency communication beacon.

Once the beacon is activated, the mission is complete.

The experience should feel like a small section of a premium sci-fi exploration game.

Do not attempt to create a huge open world. Create one compact but highly detailed and believable playable level.

---

# 2. TECHNOLOGY STACK

Use the following technologies:

- React 19
- Vite
- JavaScript (ES6+)
- Three.js
- React Three Fiber
- @react-three/drei
- Zustand for game state management
- @react-three/rapier for physics and collision detection
- CSS3 for interface design

Use compatible stable versions of all dependencies.

The project must run locally using:

npm install
npm run dev

It must also build successfully using:

npm run build

Do not introduce unnecessary backend services, APIs, authentication, or database dependencies. This is a standalone single-player game.

---

# 3. IMPORTANT: VI3W INTEGRATION

The game is being built as a demonstration project for VI3W (https://app.vi3w.in).

VI3W is a sketch-to-image-to-3D generation platform.

The purpose of this project is to demonstrate how 3D assets generated through VI3W can be incorporated into an actual interactive game.

This is extremely important.

I want to use VI3W-generated models for important game objects rather than relying entirely on primitive Three.js geometry.

### Assets to generate using VI3W

1. Main rescue rover.
2. Abandoned research facility building.
3. Energy core collectible.
4. Emergency communication beacon.
5. Industrial crates and containers.
6. Additional sci-fi equipment and environmental props.

### Asset workflow

For each asset:

1. Create a suitable concept or sketch.
2. Generate the image through VI3W.
3. Generate the corresponding 3D model.
4. Export the model in GLB format.
5. Integrate it into the React Three Fiber scene.

Use this asset directory:

public/
  assets/
    models/
      rover.glb
      facility.glb
      energy-core.glb
      beacon.glb
      crate.glb
    textures/
    audio/

### Very important asset instructions

Do not pretend to have generated models through VI3W.

Do not create fake GLB references or assume that assets exist.

Initially, build functional placeholder models using Three.js geometry.

Design the asset loading system so that I can replace those placeholder objects with actual VI3W-generated GLB files without rewriting gameplay logic.

Create reusable model components and a clear asset configuration.

When a GLB model is missing, the game must continue working with its placeholder.

Provide a README section explaining exactly how to generate, export, place, and integrate VI3W models.

---

# 4. VISUAL DESIGN DIRECTION

This is one of the most important aspects of the project.

I do not want a generic low-poly student project.

I want a realistic, cinematic, premium-looking environment.

### Environment concept

Imagine an abandoned futuristic research station in a rocky desert.

The environment should contain:

- Large rocky desert terrain.
- Uneven ground and realistic-looking rock formations.
- Abandoned industrial structures.
- Research facility buildings.
- Metallic containers and equipment.
- Damaged communication antennas.
- Abandoned machinery.
- Desert dust and atmospheric fog.
- Hazard warning lights.
- Industrial roads and pathways.
- Scattered debris and rocks.
- A distant horizon with mountains.

### Lighting

Use cinematic environmental lighting.

Implement:

- Directional sunlight.
- Hemisphere lighting.
- Ambient illumination.
- Dynamic shadows.
- Fog with carefully tuned distances.
- Emissive lighting for important mission objects.
- Subtle atmospheric particles.
- Contrast between warm environmental lighting and cool industrial lights.

The environment should have a believable atmosphere.

Avoid excessive bloom, excessive neon colors, or unnecessarily bright lighting.

### Color palette

Primary environment:

- Dusty grey.
- Warm sand.
- Dark charcoal.
- Weathered metallic surfaces.
- Muted industrial green.

Accent colors:

- Emergency lime green.
- Cyan energy lights.
- Warning amber.
- Red hazard lighting.

Use physically based materials wherever possible.

---

# 5. PLAYER AND ROVER SYSTEM

The main playable character is a futuristic four-wheel autonomous rescue rover.

The rover must look like a believable industrial machine, not a toy car.

### Rover design

- Four large off-road wheels.
- Rugged metallic chassis.
- Dark charcoal body.
- Reinforced front bumper.
- Sensor mast.
- Emergency green status lights.
- Front-mounted headlights.
- Visible suspension-inspired details.
- Industrial panels and mechanical details.

### Movement system

Implement a functional third-person rover controller.

Controls:

- W: Accelerate forward.
- S: Reverse.
- A: Steer left.
- D: Steer right.
- SPACE: Brake.
- SHIFT: Temporary speed boost.
- E: Interact with mission objects.
- ESC: Pause the game.

### Movement requirements

- Smooth acceleration.
- Gradual deceleration.
- Reverse movement.
- Smooth steering.
- Different turning behavior at low and high speeds.
- Speed limits.
- Responsive braking.
- Realistic-looking wheel rotation.
- Slight chassis movement during turning and acceleration.
- Collision detection with environmental obstacles.

Do not simply move the rover at constant speed.

Implement an arcade-style vehicle controller with believable movement characteristics.

The controller should be easy to tune through configuration constants.

### Camera system

Implement a smooth third-person follow camera.

The camera should:

- Follow behind the rover.
- Maintain a suitable distance.
- Follow the rover's turning direction.
- Smoothly interpolate between positions.
- Avoid sudden camera snapping.
- Look slightly ahead of the rover.
- Avoid clipping through nearby geometry where practical.

The camera should communicate vehicle speed and environment depth.

---

# 6. GAMEPLAY AND MISSION SYSTEM

The game must have a complete mission loop.

## Mission 01: THE LOST FACILITY

The player starts outside the research facility.

The primary objective is to collect three energy cores.

### Objective 1 — Explore the facility

The player must drive around the environment and locate the abandoned research buildings.

Display mission instructions through the HUD.

### Objective 2 — Collect three energy cores

Place three collectible objects at different locations.

Each energy core should:

- Have a unique visual appearance.
- Emit a subtle glow.
- Be recognizable from a reasonable distance.
- Have an animated idle state.
- Trigger a collection animation or particle effect.
- Update the mission counter immediately.

Interaction should be implemented reliably using a suitable detection radius or physics overlap.

HUD counter:

ENERGY CORES: 0/3

Update it as the player collects the objects.

### Objective 3 — Environmental hazards

Introduce toxic gas zones and hazardous areas.

The player should:

- Receive a warning when entering a dangerous area.
- Lose health while inside the hazard.
- See a warning indicator on the HUD.
- Be encouraged to avoid dangerous areas.

Do not make hazards unnecessarily difficult.

### Objective 4 — Restore the emergency signal

After collecting all three energy cores, the extraction beacon becomes available.

The beacon should have:

- A distinctive model.
- Animated cyan or green lighting.
- An activation interaction.
- A visible activation sequence.
- A successful mission-completion event.

The player must approach the beacon and press E.

### Mission completion

When the mission is completed:

- Play a suitable visual effect.
- Show a mission-completed screen.
- Display mission time.
- Display collected energy cores.
- Show a RESTART MISSION button.
- Allow the player to replay.

### Failure conditions

The player can fail the mission when:

- Health reaches zero.
- The mission countdown reaches zero.

Show an actual game-over screen with restart functionality.

